<div class="welcome-letter-body">
    <p>Hello! This letter includes important information; if you don't have time to read carefully now, don't worry. You will receive this letter in your email and can review it again anytime in the app on the About page.</p>

    <hr>

    <h3>First, we want to share encouraging news about the {{ $appName }} app:</h3>
    <ul class="welcome-letter-stats">
        <li>60% of the app activities are under 5 minutes,</li>
        <li>24% take 5-10 minutes,</li>
        <li>10% take 10-15 minutes,</li>
        <li>only 6% take more than 15 minutes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We designed the app this way so that you will make steady progress over four weeks to cultivate a new habit. Each Part of the {{ $appName }} app takes about 2 hours cumulative time, and you have 4 Parts to do over four weeks.</p>
    <p>You can just finish the minimum, and if you have extra time, we encourage you to repeat your favorite practices - the more you practice, the more benefits you feel.</p>

    <hr>

    <p>As a parent, when your family is affected by divorce or separation, you are holding a lot for yourself and your child. You may be managing difficult family transitions and the everyday work of parenting all at once. Divorce or separation may be part of your story, but it is not your entire story.</p>
    <p>{{ $appName }} is the first self-guided mindfulness app designed systematically for parents who are affected by divorce or separation. It was created to introduce mindfulness skills that remind you about that whole person that you are.</p>
    <p>Don't overthink the word “mindfulness”. It is not about forcing yourself to feel a certain way or denying how you are already feeling. It is simply a gentle invitation to rediscover your inner resources, including your capacity to pause, become aware, care for yourself, and bring intention to your next actions, including your interactions with your child and ex-partner.</p>
    <p>At times, this invitation may feel like another addition to your mental load.</p>
    <p>A former participant said this to us and it may be helpful to hear:</p>
    <blockquote class="welcome-letter-quote">“I think about how much time I spend on worrying or automatically scrolling on my phone, I could really spend that time on the activities in the app!”</blockquote>
    <p>By opening this app, you are making space for your well-being. That matters. The care you offer yourself can become part of the care you bring to your child.</p>
    <p class="welcome-letter-emphasis">You deserve space for you.</p>

    <p>Mindfulness is not simply a positive affirmation. Rather, it is something you come to make your own through embodied practices and experience. That is why {{ $appName }} includes opportunities to practice, explore, and discover what may be helpful for you.</p>
    <p>This inner work is not always easy. Yet even when many external circumstances are beyond your control, you can still influence how you relate and respond to them.</p>
    <p>Mindfulness can help you recognize what is within your control, accept what is not, and draw on the wisdom and strength already within you. We believe that under many difficult circumstances, the path toward greater well-being can begin by looking inwardly.</p>

    <hr>

    <h3>If you ever wonder, “Is this for me?”</h3>
    <p>That is natural, especially when an activity feels unfamiliar, challenging, or brings you into contact with difficult feelings.</p>
    <p>You might pause and ask yourself, “What kinds of support do I need to continue, and how can I get that support?” If it feels right for you, we encourage you to explore the full program before deciding what it may offer.</p>
    <p>{{ $appName }} has been tested with parents navigating separation and divorce. Their journeys are not identical to yours, but please know that you are not alone.</p>
    <p>No app can address every circumstance. Every family has its own story. Some parents have cooperative relationships and strong support systems, while others face high conflict, court proceedings, blended-family challenges, financial strain, caregiving responsibilities, or limited support. Some feel a sense of control, while others are working to rebuild their lives and regain a sense of agency after divorce.</p>
    <p>We recognize that divorce and co-parenting can be difficult and require you to navigate complex challenges that others may never understand. We have worked to make {{ $appName }} helpful to parents in many different situations.</p>
    <p>Your {{ $appName }} journey involves both learning and unlearning:</p>
    <p class="welcome-letter-learning">learning to respond with greater awareness, kindness, and intention, while gradually unlearning habitual patterns of reactivity.</p>

    <hr>

    <h3>No perfect way to practice, but your own way:</h3>
    <p>You may follow the practice as it is offered, adapt it to your needs, pause, or return another day. The goal is not to complete every activity perfectly, clear your mind, or feel calm all the time. The goal is to discover what supports you and bring it into your life in ways that feel meaningful and sustainable. Difficult feelings are normal when learning these skills, and we are proud of you for exploring them.</p>

    <h3>{{ $appName }} is a new source of support:</h3>
    <p>It is not intended to replace other kinds of support. If you need additional help including legal, financial, emotional, or medical support, that does not mean you have failed or that you cannot benefit from the {{ $appName }} app. Please take care of yourself. Reaching out to others can be important. If you ever feel unsafe or unable to cope, please seek immediate support through local crisis or emergency services.</p>

    <hr>

    <h3>Self-care is important:</h3>
    <p>Make yourself comfortable: wear loose clothing that allows easy breathing and movement, dress in layers if needed, and consider eating lightly and staying hydrated before practice.</p>
    <p>Most importantly, listen to your body. You may change positions, stand up, stretch, lie down, open your eyes, shorten a practice, or take a break at any time. If emotions or sensations become overwhelming, you may stop and return only if and when you feel ready again. Caring for yourself is not a failure to practice; it is part of the practice.</p>

    <h3>When difficult feelings or sensations arise:</h3>
    <p>During certain activities, some people notice strong emotions, memories, body sensations, or discomfort. If this ever happens to you, it does not necessarily mean you are doing anything wrong. When you pause rather than push difficult experiences away, you may become more aware of what you have been carrying. If difficult feelings arrive, practice with care and respect for your own limits. You are not alone. If you need to reach out to us, we are here to support you.</p>

    <hr>

    <h3>1+1&gt;2:</h3>
    <p><strong>You + {{ $appName }}</strong> may result in positive changes that reach beyond you and your child.</p>
    <p>We hope the app supports you. Remember, you do not need to have everything figured out before you begin. You only need to begin where you are, with this breath, this moment.</p>
    <p>We are honored to walk alongside you. Thank you for being here!</p>
</div>
